          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do I need an antibiotic every time?",
          "answer": "Antibiotics are essential for treating urinary tract infections (UTIs) caused by bacteria. They are usually necessary when:\n\n1. **Symptoms are Present**: If you experience classic symptoms of a UTI, such as a burning sensation during urination, frequent urination, urgency, or pelvic pain, antibiotics are generally required.\n\n2. **Diagnosis**: A clinician may diagnose a UTI based on symptoms and possibly confirm it with a urine test. If bacteria are found, antibiotics will usually be prescribed.\n\nHowever, antibiotics may not be needed in certain situations:\n\n1. **Asymptomatic Bacteriuria**: If bacteria are present in the urine but there are no symptoms, treatment with antibiotics is typically not recommended. This is especially true in healthy individuals.\n\n2. **Viral Infections**: If symptoms suggest a urinary infection caused by a virus (though rare), antibiotics would not be effective.\n\n3. **Mild Symptoms**: In some cases, mild symptoms may resolve without antibiotic treatment, and monitoring may be suggested.\n\nFor recurrent UTIs, your clinician may explore alternative strategies for prevention and management instead of repeated antibiotic use to avoid potential antibiotic resistance.\n\nDiscuss these considerations with your clinician to understand the best approach for your specific situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: condition-urinary-tract-infection, guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment]",
